plan Operations
title   Operations: contracts, reads, commits, write lanes and streams
status  proposed
goal
    App authors declare keyed reads, explicit commits and continuing streams. Readers share
    current data, edits survive racing reads, and every admitted wait has an honest ending.
outcomes
    O1  Contracts and keys
        resource({ id, input, output?, key, services, lookup, staleTime, gcTime, persist? }),
        transaction({ id, input, output?, key, services, concurrency, commit, progress?,
        idempotency?, guarded?, previous? }) and stream({ id, input, output, key, services,
        delivers, subscribe, position?, catchUp?, reconnect?, idleTime? }) return inert contracts,
        written in *.contracts.ts; constructing one starts no work. Contract ids follow
        @plan.Machines.O1; distinct contracts with the same id reachable from one App fail
        construction. A definition or fragment declares each use once under operations
        (@plan.Machines.O1); Runtime activation is @plan.Runtime.O2.

        input and output are each a fields record (wrapped as Schema.Struct) or a full Schema; an
        omitted output is Schema.Void; a transaction's progress has its own Schema. The function is
        inline: lookup(input, ctx) and commit(input, { attempt, basedOn, progress }) return Effects;
        subscribe(input, { from }) returns a Stream and catchUp(input, { from, next }) an Effect.
        Contexts carry no signal: Flow interrupts the function's fiber, and an adapter that needs an
        AbortSignal takes Effect's own (Effect.tryPromise's or Effect.callback's, or
        Effect.abortSignal under Effect.scoped). services lists the services the function calls,
        is required on every contract and is checked against the function's R (a missing one is a
        type error); the Runtime's boot refuses by name an operation whose service its Layer lacks
        (@plan.Runtime.O2). Value, error and service types remain intact through declarations and
        use. Persisted resources require output; domain reads require it under
        @plan.EntityDomains.O1; boundary transfer is @plan.ServerRendering.O3. Typed errors never
        cross those boundaries. Resource persist defaults to false; transactions and streams have
        no persist option. Storage is @plan.Persistence.O2.

        staleTime and gcTime are required nonnegative Duration inputs; invalid values fail
        construction. Zero means immediately stale or collected at the next turn boundary after the
        last hold ends. Infinity disables time-based staleness or collection, respectively.
        concurrency is required and is reject, latest, cancel or serialize (@O3). A contract that
        moves money keys the server by something it recognises: idempotency: "attempt" or the input
        fields it is, so flow check can warn on a money-shaped write keyed by the Attempt; a server
        may prune keys, so a re-send on an old key reads first, and Flow keeps no key age.
        guarded: true on a transaction lets a proof of the record's version end a quiet doubt
        (@plan.EntityDomains.O1).

        key is required on every contract: a list of top-level input fields naming which server
        thing a call is about, [] for one; a composite key lists every field. Its Schema is input's
        pick; there are no derived or nested keys and no default. A key field is required and has
        no default (KeyFieldOptional otherwise). Equal key records identify the same resource entry,
        write lane or stream session within one Runtime, regardless of field order. Any value
        carrying the key's fields names the key; extra fields are ignored and a missing field is a
        type error. On a resource every input field that changes the output belongs in key
        (KeyOmitsInput warns otherwise). A key is stored as canonical text; a value JSON cannot
        carry gets a stable name, so a bigint key 1n restores as "$1n" and names the same lane. A
        key's fingerprint (field names, and each field's Schema type side and encoded side) is part
        of the operation's version (@O3, @plan.Persistence.O5). Picked key values accept null,
        booleans, finite numbers, strings, dense arrays and plain records. Undefined record
        properties are absent; undefined array items, cycles, accessors, non-plain objects, lone
        surrogates and unsupported values are rejected without running getters or coercion.
        Negative zero equals zero; Unicode spelling matters. Keys allow at most 16 nested levels,
        256 value nodes and 8,192 UTF-8 bytes of canonical text. Invalid keys report InvalidKey with
        the offending path and change nothing before work starts; a key that cannot be encoded is
        @O3's Unencodable. Later mutation of input cannot change an admitted key.

        invalidate and clear match a contract plus a partial key: every named property must equal
        the entry's whole property value; {} matches the family. Keys are exposed in persistence,
        payloads and traces; they neither authorize work nor reconstruct input. An author error
        whose tag is one of Flow's reasons (@O3) is a type error, refused at compile as
        ReservedReason (proposed name) wherever a failure tag is declared.

        Superseded: "resource({ id, key?, lookup, result?, staleTime, gcTime, persist? }),
        transaction({ id, key?, input?, commit, concurrency }) and stream({ id, key?, subscribe,
        result?, persist? })" is replaced by the contract shapes above: input and output, a required
        key, services (C31; C86; C138). "lookup(params, { signal }) and commit(params, { signal,
        attempt, basedOn }) return Effects; subscribe(params, { signal }) returns a Stream." is
        replaced by the same contexts without signal (C116). "concurrency is required and is reject,
        cancel, allow or serialize" is replaced by reject, latest, cancel or serialize; allow is cut
        from v1 (C153). "key(params) is a pure synchronous record projector; omission means {}." is
        replaced by the required field list (C31). "persist defaults to false; transactions have no
        persist option" now excludes streams too (C86). "In actions they reject the batch; in
        activities they report an issue and leave the lane undefined; passive reads throw." is
        narrowed: an unencodable key declines or ends Defect (C154). "Authors must include every
        parameter affecting the result" becomes KeyOmitsInput (C31).
    O2  Resources and reads
        Equal contract and key share data and one in-flight lookup across actors in a Runtime. An
        entry is fresh only while it has confirmed data, is not invalidated and its age is less than
        staleTime. Separate Runtimes share nothing.

        O.x.lookup(select) returns fresh data without a request, joins an in-flight lookup, or
        starts one, and points the operation's one lane at that entry. A one-off read operation has
        one lane (in a fragment, one per incarnation); a newer lookup releases the previous one,
        whose settlement is evidence (Superseded), so only the latest lookup's ending is delivered.
        Its row is @O6's resource lane for the entry the latest lookup named, with that entry's key,
        held until the next lookup or cancel, undefined before the first lookup and after cancel.
        One-off read lanes are not persisted, except the Interrupted ending a freeze or an actor's
        end parks for each read it stops. O.x.refetch(k?) starts a request when none is running.
        While one runs, refetch joins it if it started after the entry last became stale
        (invalidate, write or clear); otherwise one trailing request starts when it settles and the
        caller joins that. An entry never has more than one running and one trailing request, so a
        poll faster than its server still lands data. O.x.subscribe(select, { when? }) places the
        operation and starts a request only when missing or stale with none running. Time passing
        alone starts nothing. Automatic requests use the oldest live placement's input; explicit
        requests use the caller's. A running request retains its starting input even if that
        placement leaves. The verb must agree with the mapping: success only with lookup, value
        only with subscribe; one operation uses one verb (VerbMismatch).

        Focus regained and connection restored refetch stale or missing subscribed entries with no
        request running, ordered by contract id then key. Offline status blocks and cancels nothing.
        Invalidation and clear follow @O5; domain repair follows @plan.EntityDomains.O1. Flow never
        automatically retries a failed lookup.

        A read has two channels, success and failure. Its success: and value: see confirmed data
        only; success carries current data even when ordering rejects its request's result, and
        equal data still settles the lookup. A read's defect or interruption ends on failure as
        Flow's Defect or Interrupted, which a tag record must map; a defect is also an actor issue.
        A read whose mapper throws, or returns something with no shape, ends on failure as Defect,
        with the store's UserClosure issue. The failure mapping is exhaustive over error tags, or
        one event for an untagged error. A mapping value may be Flow.ignore: no event and no
        handler; never on a stream key or a listen entry. Mappers receive (value, { input, key }),
        key being the contract's key record of the call that ended. An ending is owned by the
        activation of the state its transition names and routed current or late at admission
        (@plan.Machines.O6). A read declared .gone(tag) with .byKey turns that failure tag into a
        tombstone for its key's row (@plan.EntityDomains.O1). Unmapped failure leaves its lane tag
        and an issue.

        value fires for confirmed data at placement and subsequently only when it changes by
        Equal.equals; optimistic values and predictions never fire it. Restored or hydrated
        placements do not remap an already mapped value (@plan.Persistence.O2,
        @plan.ServerRendering.O5). A live placement, request or optimistic layer holds an entry, and
        a one-off read operation holds its latest entry. After its last hold, gcTime on the Runtime
        Clock removes it; later use starts missing. Releasing one owner preserves shared work until
        the last owner leaves. Passive reads never hold or start work. Cleared, collected or
        replaced requests cannot later change data or replacement placements.

        Superseded: "Each finite lookup or refetch settles once as success(A), failure, defect or
        interrupt" is replaced by two channels with Defect and Interrupted on failure, and for
        lookups through one operation by "only its latest lookup is delivered" (C5; C29; C32).
        "State exit does not cancel finite work; its event reaches the actor's later state." is
        replaced by the owner test (C12; C162; C170). "Actor disposal ends delivery." is replaced by
        the actor's end parking Interrupted (C143). "Continuing outcomes are value(A), failure,
        defect and interrupt." is replaced by value and failure (C5). "Each outcomes member is
        optional; tagged failure mappings must cover every error tag, untagged mappings receive E,
        and E = never forbids failure." keeps its failure rule; Defect and Interrupted must be
        mapped in a tag record (C29). "Mappers receive values only." is replaced by (value, { input,
        key })
        (C6). "Unmapped failure or defect" loses defect (C5).
    O3  Transactions, commits and write lanes
        Only an accepted action admits O.x.commit(select, { optimistic? }); activation, restore,
        hydration and state re-entry never replay a commit. select reads the turn's after memory and
        the event. optimistic, the commit's only option, reads the starting turn's after memory,
        captured at start, and applies on start; writes is the operation's option,
        contract.outcomes(mapping, { writes }), receives { value, input }, reads no memory and
        applies only on success, before its ending's event, even after the issuing state exits.
        Domain operations have no optimistic, and their writes may only invalidate.

        Endings. A commit ends in one of three channels: success (it happened), failure (this send
        definitely did not do it) or unknown (it may have happened). The commit function classifies
        each ending with Outcome.success, Outcome.failure or Outcome.unknown; a plain error is a
        failure; a throw or die is unknown with reason Defect, carrying the Cause, and an actor
        issue. Flow interprets no status and no error type. The runtime classifies each send from
        the commit fiber's Exit and whole Cause, outside that fiber, Flow's own knowledge first,
        then most cautious first. The ending delivered is the Attempt's: a definite failure after an
        unknown send of the same Attempt is unknown, StillUnknown { failure }. Flow's reasons are
        reserved: the failure NotSent { why } (Cancelled, Restored, Rejected, Overloaded,
        StorageRefused, Disposed, NoPersistence, OldVersion, Unencodable, ModuleDisabled), and the
        unknowns Cancelled, Restored, Interrupted, Defect, StillUnknown { failure }, StorageRefused,
        Disposed and Abandoned. ModuleDisabled and Abandoned are proposed names. Superseded,
        NothingToResend, InFlight and Released are evidence, never endings. Flow has no timeout
        reason: a call's deadline is its commit's Effect (Effect.timeoutOrElse).

        Mapping. The operation maps each ending onto a declarer event (@plan.Machines.O1): success:,
        failure: (an exhaustive record over the commit's failure tags, NotSent optional in the type,
        or one event for an untagged failure) and unknown: (one event, or a record by tag, whose
        byReason keys must be the commit's own unknown tags or Flow's unknown reasons). Mappers
        receive (value, { input, attempt, progress, key }); a NotSent mapper's attempt is optional
        (a declined commit has none). Every unknown: target is a Flow.uncertain event or
        Flow.ignore. A bare operation maps nothing: its endings are evidence and its unknown is not
        retained. An unknown that is not retained (bare, ignored, or a cancel's own turn end) is
        released, and the store hears Released (@O5). flow check requires NotSent mapped only where
        it can occur (@plan.CommandLine.O5).

        The Attempt. Every admitted commit has an exported Attempt { id, operation, key, input,
        retryCount, mayHaveApplied }. Flow mints id, random, when the write starts; every send of
        that write is the same Attempt with the same id and input. The commit author puts id on the
        wire where the API expects it; Flow sends nothing itself and derives no other key.
        retryCount counts invoked re-sends; mayHaveApplied says an earlier send ended unknown. A
        definite failure resolves the Attempt: trying again is a new write with a new id. Input uses
        the input Schema. Domain prediction versions arrive as basedOn under @plan.EntityDomains.O1;
        otherwise {}. Attempt durability is @plan.Persistence.O6.

        The write lane. A write lane is (actor identity, contract id, key), owned by the Runtime,
        not the actor; it is created by work and removed when Idle. Its binding incarnation (the
        actor, or for a fragment's operation the live incarnation with the lane's subject path)
        receives its endings and its members address it. States: Idle; InFlight, one addressable
        Attempt waiting to be invoked or on the wire; Held, a retained unknown; each with queued
        commits W (none under reject, at most one under latest, a FIFO of at most 64 under
        serialize). A turn's members apply to the lanes in action order in its validation step.
        commit: Idle mints an Attempt; InFlight or Held, reject ends NotSent { Rejected } and the
        transition runs, latest queues one commit (a previous queued commit, or a never-sent head
        not yet invoked, ends Superseded, evidence), serialize appends to W and past 64 ends
        NotSent { Overloaded }. Under concurrency cancel a newer commit interrupts the previous one,
        whose ending is evidence. retry(k?) re-sends a Held Attempt with its id and input unchanged
        (InFlight: evidence InFlight; Idle or resolved: evidence NothingToResend). When the
        addressable Attempt is resolved (success, failure) or released, W's head is promoted; an
        unknown, a retry and a restore never promote. Every Attempt minted, promoted or re-sent is
        invoked after its turn ends, whatever the turn's outcome, once its acknowledgement, record
        queues and (for a persisted re-send) its lane record's capture allow. Under reject, latest
        and serialize an unknown holds the lane until retry resolves it or cancel releases it; no
        quiet release, no automatic retry. A kept ending (refused twice, @plan.Runtime.O4) blocks
        its key until heard or let go: idle denies, a commit declines NotSent { Rejected }, cancel
        lets it go. Two write operations over one contract in one actor tree are a SharedContract
        error, so a lane per contract and a lane per operation name the same lane within a release.

        Members: commit, retry(k?), cancel(k?) and cancelAll(k?) in actions; idle(k?) (InFlight:
        denies InFlight; Held: denies Uncertain; a partial key passes only when every lane it
        matches is Idle) and retryable(k?, { times }) (passes in Held while retryCount < times,
        else RetryLimit { retryCount }; denies InFlight or NothingToResend) in guards; get. On an
        unkeyed operation (key []) a key-less member names its one lane in every handler, a NotSent
        ending's included. On a keyed operation retry, retryable and cancel may omit the key only in
        a handler of an event that operation alone maps, naming that ending's own Attempt only; once
        it is resolved or released a key-less member acts as on an Idle lane; under serialize, only
        in the head's own ending's handler; elsewhere a key-less member is KeyRequired. idle and get
        always take a key on a keyed operation. Starting is static: a write starts only from
        actions.

        Delivery. Every commit, and every retry that sends, delivers exactly one ending to a live
        incarnation of its identity with the lane's subject path, or parked for it; delivery is
        counted per ending: a re-send whose lane-record capture is refused delivers a second ending
        of the same Attempt, unknown { StorageRefused }, and re-parking an ending is not a second
        delivery. A settled ending is never rewritten. Evidence by declaration: a replaced
        never-sent commit, a released lookup, a declined retry, a bare write's endings, an ending
        mapped to Flow.ignore, under concurrency cancel the interrupted Attempt's ending, a
        fragment's lookup whose incarnation ended, and a releasing lane's endings and queued
        commits. A throw from the outcome mapper at admission refuses the turn, and the ending is
        redelivered once after queued mail. A late handler may start a commit; it is an ordinary
        call owned by its transition's state. Compensation recipe: handle a late success at an
        ancestor by moving an idle state to a compensating state that starts the undo; try the
        undo's definite failures again as new writes after a wait, and reconcile its unknowns with
        O.x.retry of the same Attempt, each with a budget. Flow promises no remote rollback and
        never retries automatically.

        Persistence and identity. Restore and reload are @plan.Persistence.O4 and O6: a first send's
        capture and acknowledgement are one batch with the turn that committed it, so a kill before
        it lands restores the state before that turn with no ending; an Attempt acknowledged once
        restores as unknown { Restored }, delivered once per new incarnation (a reload, or a return
        after an end); queued commits end NotSent { Restored }; a head whose own ending is parked
        restores as that ending, never also unknown { Restored }. A persisted identity's commit in a
        Runtime without persistence ends NotSent { NoPersistence }. Runtime disposal is
        @plan.Runtime.O6 (past its bound an Attempt still on the wire ends unknown { Disposed }). A
        reset of the actor's record keeps its writes in flight: lanes, holds and parked endings stay
        unbound until an adopting ensure. A lane is (identity with its version, contract id, key):
        a lane of an older identity version, or of an older key fingerprint, is an old-version hold;
        until it ends every commit of that operation for that identity declines
        NotSent { OldVersion } under every policy, and a keyed idle denies; the contract declares
        earlier keys, previous: [{ key }], giving O.x.previous[i].retry(k), .cancel(k) and
        .retryable(k, { times }), which name only previous[i]'s lanes; a current member never names
        an old-version lane; a previous key equal to the current one is refused (PreviousIsCurrent,
        proposed name); on a fragment operation another path's Rejected comes before OldVersion.

        Values with no form never fail a turn: a commit whose input or key cannot encode declines
        NotSent { Unencodable }, its transition runs and its mapper receives the value the turn
        asked to send. Domain writes: their store effects publish at settlement, before their
        ending is queued; a commit past its lane's queue, any record queue, or a many key array past
        4,096 items (counted from the write's targets before any join, repeats included) ends
        NotSent { Overloaded } with no prediction and no partial join, the transition and its other
        actions running; a domain write joins its record queues when its Attempt heads its lane,
        never at commit (@plan.EntityDomains.O1); a store module's defect declines a write that
        would join NotSent { ModuleDisabled } until reload.

        Superseded: "Outcomes are success(A), failure, defect, interrupt and unknown(Attempt), with
        @O2 mappings." and the descriptor's failureKinds are replaced by three channels (C1).
        "select reads the pre-turn snapshot and event" is replaced by the after memory (C13).
        "O.x.commit(select, { optimistic?, writes?, outcomes? })" is replaced by optimistic as the
        only commit option and writes and outcomes on the operation (C15; C37). "unencodable input
        rejects admission before work starts" is replaced by NotSent { Unencodable } (C154).
        "Concurrency is per actor, descriptor and key" is replaced by the write lane (actor
        identity, contract id, key) (C33; C42). "reject declines a second unsettled commit without
        work or outcome; the action continues." is replaced by NotSent { Rejected } with the
        transition running (C10). "allow starts every commit independently and delivers every
        outcome in settlement order." is cut from v1 (C153). "serialize starts commits FIFO,
        applying optimistic writes only on start; at most 64 queued occurrences are allowed.
        Overflow rejects the whole action batch with AdmissionOverloaded before any work starts." is
        replaced by W's 64 bound ending NotSent { Overloaded } for that write only (C10; C22).
        "Every invoked commit delivers exactly one outcome." is replaced by "every commit and every
        retry that sends ends once", counted per ending (C11; C165). "Before invocation, an explicit
        cancel ends it silently, while a failed Attempt acknowledgment or a never-invoked restore
        ends it as interrupt." is replaced by NotSent { Cancelled }, NotSent { StorageRefused } and
        NotSent { Restored } (C11; C48; C128). "Its optimistic changes and success writes publish
        together before its event" becomes, for a domain write, publication at settlement before its
        ending is queued (C21). "During actor disposal, cache consequences still apply but outcome
        events are dropped" is replaced by Runtime-owned lanes and parked endings (C33; C43). "Once
        the adapter has been called, cancellation or abandonment reports unknown with its Attempt"
        is kept as unknown { Cancelled } and unknown { Disposed } (C11; C158). "Restored work with
        no acknowledged Attempt cannot have been invoked and ends interrupt" is replaced by the one
        batch with its turn (C128). "work with an acknowledged Attempt restores as unknown unless a
        durable settled result is known" becomes unknown { Restored } once per incarnation, a parked
        ending restoring as itself (C126; C167). "O.x.retry(select, options) accepts an Attempt and
        reuses its exact id and decoded input under the same concurrency, writes and outcome rules.
        A known settled result settles immediately with its consequences and no adapter call. A
        foreign descriptor or invalid input rejects the action batch with InvalidAttempt." is
        replaced by retry(k?) in Held only; InvalidAttempt is removed (C7; C23). Descriptor-level
        cancel and pending are removed (C7). "Flow promises no remote rollback and never retries
        automatically" is kept (C7).
    O4  Streams
        A stream contract declares delivers: "every" (never skipped or silenced, one per turn, or
        one page per turn from a read) or "latest" (newest wins, current only while this connection
        confirmed it); position names a field of output whose value says where each message is
        (every only); catchUp reads history from the bookmark (every only, requires position);
        reconnect: { while?, schedule? } declares that Flow re-runs a failed connection; idleTime
        (required exactly with reconnect, "Infinity" for none) fails a connection that says nothing
        within it. A stream's resume position is not an input field.

        Placement: O.x.subscribe(select) or O.x.subscribe.each(select) in activities. A session is
        one per (actor incarnation, placement path, operation, key); its select runs on every
        committed turn to find the key, and the session reads its input once, at start, so a
        non-key field never restarts it. A select that throws keeps what is placed, with one
        UserClosure issue each turn; one returning undefined (under .each, not a list) releases what
        was placed, with one SelectedNoKey issue (proposed name). A session belongs to its
        placement's activation; a released session's undelivered items are discarded with evidence,
        and a new session for the same (actor, operation, key) waits for a releasing predecessor.
        Two operations on one contract and key are two sessions, with a DuplicateSession warning.
        Flow runs connections only by the declared reconnect and idleTime, and sessions only by
        placement; it never re-opens a session. The default schedule is exponential from 500 ms,
        jittered by a factor of 0.8 to 1.2 from a well-mixed hash of the session id and step (no
        saved seed), capped at 30 s; it retries while offline and ends Flapping after 8 registered
        flaps. Sessions release within Runtime O6's 5 s bound (ReleaseOverdue).

        The adapter says three things: a value arrived; the server is listening (Feed.live(), with
        { proof } that nothing was missed, { head } giving the newest position, or { restartAt } for
        a restarted log); the connection dropped (Feed.down(reason)); Feed.ping is a sign of life.
        Positions in signals decode against the position's Schema at intake; a failure is Defect.
        Feed.callback<A, E, R>(register) is shaped as Stream.callback and bounded at 1,024 queued
        items (the delivery in the mailbox not counted). Feed.multiplex({ connect }), library code
        beside it, holds one connection while any key has a session: add and remove on a key's
        first and last session, emit.to(key, x), emit.all(x), emit.fail(e), emit.fail(e, key)
        (the runner's addition) and hub.stream(key); a joiner of a registered key hears
        Feed.live() at once; the hub lives in the app's Layer per Runtime. A catchUp answers
        Feed.more({ items, next, through? }), Feed.done({ items, proof, through? }) or
        Feed.gone({ reason, items?, next?, through? }).

        Outcomes: every with a machine consumer maps value (required), pages (optional, needs
        catchUp), missed (required: Unproven, Lost or Reset, exactly one notice per interruption),
        fills (required: a memory field or false) and failure (required, exhaustive over E, with
        Feed covering Flow's Closed, Overflow, Silent, Flapping, Defect and Interrupted); latest
        maps value optionally and failure when anything is mapped. Delivery is at least once:
        handlers store by id. Deliveries are bounded by what Flow has pulled, outside the mailbox
        cap.

        The bookmark is a Flow-private partition of the slice's saved record, per (operation, key),
        holding at and filled, and for a latest operation last and heard; it is not a memory field,
        has no patch and no guard reads it. Flow moves it when a value's turn is admitted, keeps it
        through Denied and Unhandled, and a Denied value moves it with DeliveryDenied. At most 256
        entries per operation, the oldest by last write dropped; a re-created entry takes filled
        from the field. fills names a field of the operation's own memory (an array, a Record or a
        number): emptied while the session stays placed it is cleared (stays cleared), emptied by a
        turn that released the session or while unplaced it is wiped (catchUp re-reads; without it,
        missed). A handler that changes a placed session's key while the state stays put empties
        the fills field in the same turn; Flow never wipes it; FillsOtherKey (proposed name) flags a
        registration with no entry beside a filled field and another key's filled entry.
        O.x.refetch(k?) forgets the bookmark and reads now; O.x.bookmark(select) states a position.
        Waiting has no connection; dropAt is set on leaving Live or Repairing and when none is set,
        kept across connections that never register; a registration ends the not-live period, and
        dropAt is kept with a latest bookmark. Domain listeners follow @plan.EntityDomains.O1.
        Persisted bookmarks and reopening follow @plan.Persistence.O4.

        Superseded: the stream text "Each activity owns its session. ... Persisted latest values and
        session reopening follow @plan.Persistence.O4." is rewritten from the streams rules (C87).
        "A second same-actor session for the same descriptor and key reports DuplicateStreamSession,
        leaves its lane undefined and preserves the existing session." is replaced by two sessions
        with a DuplicateSession warning (C87). "Equal values are silent." is replaced by
        at-least-once delivery (C87). "complete, failure, defect and adapter interrupt end the
        session once, retain its data and map their optional outcomes. A terminal session restarts
        only when its activity starts again" is replaced by declared reconnect and the outcome table
        (C87; C90). "Changing identity releases it before opening another" gains the wait for a
        releasing predecessor (C87). "a session reads input once" gains the select on every turn
        (C201).
    O5  Writes and optimistic values
        O.x.setData(select) returns { key, data }; data is a value or updater(current) => A |
        undefined. Updaters read confirmed data; undefined declines the write.
        O.x.invalidate(select) and O.x.clear(select) return partial keys. A source replaces data
        only if its request started at or after the stored data landed. setData starts and lands
        when applied, so older lookups cannot overwrite it. Accepted equal writes refresh freshness
        without value events. A commit's optimistic and a setData action on its own entries are type
        errors on a domain operation.

        A write batch validates all targets before mutation and publishes atomically. More than 256
        expanded identities (AdmissionOverloaded) or any invalid target (InvalidKey) rejects the
        entire batch; no match is a successful no-op. invalidate retains data and optimistic values,
        marks entries stale and refetches subscribed entries with no request running. A request
        started before invalidation cannot make the entry fresh. clear removes data and optimistic
        values, blocks old results and makes surviving placements Pending before reacquiring.
        Related commits still settle. Domain write restrictions and predictions are
        @plan.EntityDomains.O1.

        Every reader sees confirmed data with live optimistic updates applied in start order. Base
        changes and removal of one update preserve the others; predicted remains true while any
        update is live. Optimistic values never become confirmed data or cross persistence or
        hydration boundaries. The Attempt's ending decides: success confirms the optimistic look;
        failure (NotSent included) removes that commit's updates without a refetch and without
        overwriting newer data; unknown keeps them, marked uncertain, and refetches the displayed
        entries once. The refetch is a read: if it fails the look stays marked uncertain and the
        next natural trigger reads again; Flow never loops, and revertOnUnknown does not exist. An
        unknown that is not retained is Released to the store, and a release before the forced
        repair removes nothing. Success retains the updates until setData or a lookup started after
        settlement confirms the entry. clear removes them with the entry.

        Superseded: "pending remains true while any update is live" is replaced by predicted (C27).
        "Failure, defect, interrupt and pre-invocation cancellation remove that commit's updates" is
        replaced by the Attempt's three channels (C1; C4). "unknown retains them and invalidates the
        entry until a confirming lookup lands" is replaced by "keeps them, marked uncertain, and
        refetches the displayed entries once" (C4). Released is added (C113).
    O6  Rows
        s.operations.<name> reflects that snapshot's publication and keeps object identity until its
        lane, entry or session changes; inside a fragment it is the fragment's own rows. A write
        row is { pending, uncertain, waiting, retryCount, progress? }, plus outstanding under
        serialize, and { pending, retryCount, progress? } under cancel: pending is InFlight,
        uncertain is Held, waiting is W not empty, outstanding counts unresolved commits other than
        the addressable Attempt, retryCount counts the addressable Attempt's invoked re-sends, and
        progress is set by the commit's progress(p) with no turn. An unkeyed write's row is plain;
        a keyed write's s.operations.<name> is a keyed list of the lanes that exist, each { key,
        ...row }, read by key with O.x.get(s, k), which is total: a key with no lane reads the idle
        row. A one-off read's row is undefined before its first lookup and after cancel. A live
        placement's row is undefined while not placed; under subscribe.each it is a keyed list read
        by key. Guards and callbacks read rows with O.x.get(operations, k) as views do. Resource
        tags are Pending (no confirmed data), Ready (confirmed data, no request), Refreshing
        (confirmed data with a request), Failure (typed error), Defect and Interrupted. Failure,
        Defect and Interrupted retain previous data; Refreshing always has data. All carry data and
        predicted, true for live optimistic values or predictions, and key. A stream's row, latest:
        key, connection, drop, failure, value (empty unless current) and last { value, at }; every:
        key, connection, drop, failure and catchingUp; the bookmark is in session records and
        Inspection, not on the row. Undefined emissions need an author wrapper if they must be
        distinguished from no value. Domain row additions are @plan.EntityDomains.O1.

        placeholder({ previous, input }) on a placement may supply Pending data when a new key has
        no data; placeholder: true marks it. previous is this actor's placement's last input and
        lane data, retained across exit and re-entry. Placeholders never enter the store or
        persistence. contract.get(snapshot, input) (the plan's descriptor.get, renamed with
        descriptors; proposed name) passively returns the lane-shaped view at that snapshot's store
        revision, or undefined. It creates, holds, refreshes and starts nothing;
        invalid keys throw.

        Superseded: "snapshot.activities.<name>" is replaced by s.operations.<name> (C35; C41).
        "Transactions have outcomes, never lanes." is replaced: transactions have lanes (C35).
        "pending, true for live optimistic values or predictions" is renamed predicted (C27).
        "Stream tags are Running, Complete, Failure, Defect and Interrupted; data is the latest
        value or undefined, with no pending field." is replaced by the stream rows (C88). "It is
        undefined for inactive activities, when false, or null, undefined or false parameters"
        becomes undefined while not placed (C40). "A stream read sees only that snapshot actor's
        session" is kept as the row per placement (C88). "descriptor.get" reads contract.get (C31).
    O7  Cancellation and cleanup
        Planned release interrupts the fiber and runs finalizers once without interrupt endings;
        adapter-reported interruption of a read ends Interrupted. Releasing a placement stops shared
        work only at its last owner. O.x.cancel(k?) acts on the lane's addressable Attempt, the one
        retry names: on the wire it interrupts the fiber, and the ending unknown { Cancelled } is
        queued when the fiber exits and retained (Held), so nothing newer is sent while the request
        may land; waiting to be invoked it acts by history (never sent: NotSent { Cancelled }, and
        the next queued commit is promoted; a re-send returns to Held and is released); in Held it
        releases (Released, evidence) and promotes; in Idle it does nothing, with evidence. While a
        never-sent Attempt's acknowledgement is being written, a cancel waits for storage: stored,
        it ends NotSent { Cancelled }; refused, NotSent { StorageRefused }. A latest or
        cancel-concurrency replacement waiting on that acknowledgement ends the replaced head
        Superseded with StorageFailure evidence when refused, and the newer commit is acknowledged
        and sent; a pending cancel is never lost to a later replacement. That wait is unbounded, a
        stated limit. O.x.cancelAll(k?) ends every queued commit NotSent { Cancelled } in mint
        order, then acts on the head as cancel does, promoting nothing; a Held lane ends Idle; the
        head's ending, which leaves a hold, is delivered last. cancel on a one-off read releases the
        operation's wait, not the shared request; resources keep no cancel of the entry.

        cancel never drops a queued commit silently, with one exception: a fragment's release on
        leave (a key-less O.x.cancel in whenAbandoned) marks every lane of that operation for the
        ending incarnation's subject path releasing, interrupts an Attempt on the wire, ends queued
        commits as evidence, and applies later endings to the lane and store without delivering
        them. A release wins over cancelAll in the same transition. One write operation per contract
        per actor holds across placements. Actor disposal invokes nothing new and drops nothing:
        lanes stay with the Runtime (@plan.Runtime.O6). Cleanup failure reports an issue without
        undoing a published transition. Disposal failure follows @plan.Runtime.O6; Cause handling
        follows @plan.Diagnostics.O3. After cleanup, late results cannot change data, lanes,
        endings or replacement work.

        Superseded: "Planned release aborts the signal and runs finalizers once" is replaced by
        "interrupts the fiber" (C116). "O.x.cancel(select) cancels this actor's queued and running
        commits for a key." is replaced by cancel on the addressable Attempt (C11; C36). "Resources
        have no cancel action." is amended by cancel on a one-off read (C36). "Before invocation,
        cancellation is silent and removes optimistic updates; after invocation it reports unknown
        and blocks late results." is replaced by NotSent { Cancelled } and a retained
        unknown { Cancelled } (C11). "disposed actors receive no finite lookup outcome" and "Actor
        disposal waits for invoked commits; bounded disposal follows @plan.Runtime.O6." are replaced
        by Runtime-owned lanes (C43; C158). The release on leave is added (C68; C183); cancelAll is
        added (C171); the acknowledgement wait is added (C146; C180; C186).
verification
    V1  Type proofs preserve adapter types and reject invalid options, outcome mappings, reserved
        reasons, missing services and key-less members of keyed operations.
    V2  Key tests prove equality, matching, canonical text, bounds and rejection before any side
        effect.
    V3  Stories prove shared reads, one-off read delivery, Runtime isolation and passive snapshot
        reads.
    V4  Clock Stories prove freshness, collection, refetch triggers and late-result rejection.
    V5  Stories prove atomic batch limits, invalidation repair and edits surviving older reads.
    V6  Stories prove each concurrency policy's lane cells, queue limits and declines, and cache
        writes before ending handlers.
    V7  Stories prove optimistic stacking, independent removal and confirmation after success.
    V8  Stories prove unknown holds, retry preserving the Attempt, cancel by history, cancelAll
        order, no restore replay and old-version holds.
    V9  Stories prove the Streams tables (session, registration, bookmark, delivery, latest,
        Feed.callback's queue, listen coverage, write progress, lifecycle) and their oracles.
    V10  Stories prove rows, retained data, placeholder isolation and historical reads.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Choose key encoding and validation mechanics. Choose internal request and settlement
        identities and capacity accounting. Choose Attempt id generation and structural input codec
        details. Choose store layout, batch traversal and publication mechanics. Choose cleanup
        instrumentation. Stream buffering is decided by the Streams delivery and Feed.callback
        tables (C89); host-signal wiring is decided: adapters take Effect's own signal (C116).
